Speed and velocity are two fundamental concepts in physics, primarily used to describe the rate at which objects change their position or change their velocity.

Speed is a measure of the rate at which an object is moving or changing its position. It is a scalar quantity, meaning it has no direction or magnitude. It is always measured in units of distance or time. For example, a car is moving at 50 miles per hour.

Velocity, on the other hand, is a measure of the rate of change of the position of an object with respect to time. It is a scalar quantity that can take on any value in the interval of time it represents. Velocity can be positive (moving forward) or negative (moving backward), depending on the direction of the velocity.

To illustrate the difference, consider a person who runs at 6 miles per hour while walking. In this case, the person's speed is positive, indicating that they are moving forward. However, the person's velocity is negative, indicating that their speed is constantly changing direction.

The example I provided earlier, where a car is running at 50 miles per hour, illustrates both concepts. The car is moving forward at a constant speed, while the person's velocity is changing direction every instant, as they move forward.
